  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/e/e9/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_53.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "While demonstrating for an orphanage, a concealed Porcupine shoots Giant-Man, causing him to fall and fracture an ankle (we learn here that his giant form weighs 360 pounds).&nbsp; He's still seething about being stopped in [LINK:1963:TTA_48_ant0|TTA #48|2026-11-22].&nbsp; He joins up with the local Giant-Man and Wasp fan club and plans to join them when they visit Giant-Man, now laid up with the hurt ankle.&nbsp; They'll all wear costumes of his old villains, except Porcupine's will be the real thing!\n\nThe unsuspecting Giant-Man and Wasp let the fan club right on in (<i>how on earth did the kids know where to look</i>?).&nbsp; Porcupine sends the Wasp out to his car to pick up a \"gift,\" and the moment she leaves, he floods the room with sleeping gas.&nbsp; Hank is hampered by the ankle, but he's above the gas.&nbsp; However, Porcupine tells him that the car is booby-trapped, and that he'll hold the Wasp prisoner - Giant-Man needs to leave him alone!\n\nHe hobbles down to the car, imagining how Jan was trapped, but not in time to see it zoom off!&nbsp; Porcupine locks Jan up, demanding that she tell him Giant-Man's true identity.&nbsp; Hank tries to find him with ants, but he's equipped his stuff with DDT, which prevents the ants from reporting back.&nbsp; But Porcupine actually <i>wants</i> Jan to escape, and he'll follow her back to their secret headquarters.&nbsp; She does, and P tracks her to their New Jersey home.\n\nWhen Jan finally arrives, Hank opens up about how he didn't realize how much he needed her.&nbsp; But P has done it - he's tracked her back to their HQ!&nbsp; Soon he crashes in and hits Giant-Man with sleeping gas, as he's still weakened by the ankle injury.&nbsp; As they tussle, P tries to grab at the pills in Giant-Man's belt and is able to get some, which he pops in his mouth, but he's taken the wrong ones!&nbsp; He begins shrinking, and because he swallowed more than one, he keeps shrinking, apparently to microbe size and vanishes!\n\nThe day is saved.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "Oh <i>barf!</i>  I'm really speechless here.&nbsp; <i>Tales to Astonish</i> remains the most gag-inducing book that Marvel publishes (at least since the <i>Hulk</i> was discontinued).&nbsp; The villain was lame the first time, and this only made it worse, bringing him back at all.&nbsp; The \"fractured\" ankle of <i>course</i> is healed up by the time the issue is over.&nbsp; The plot was just downright boring and unimaginative.&nbsp; I hope to never see this Porcupine character again, but alas, I know we shall, which is really a shame.&nbsp; He's an idiot.\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nLet's face it folks. If you're missing this issue in your collection, you're not missing anything!",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>The Wonderful Wasp Tells a Tale:</b>  \"When Wakes the Colossus!\"\n\nBefore Jan leaves for the orphans, she gives Hank a preview of the tale she's about to spin, which is about a warlord named Mingo on a far-off planet, intent to prey on more primitive races on their world.&nbsp; Their conquest of Asikii race goes awry when a stone colossus erected to keep the Asikii in constant fear instead mysteriously awakens and attacks the conquerors, all thanks to the fact that, though \"<i>mortals</i> may be evil, but the <i>supernatural powers are not</i>!\"\n\nJan gets mad at Hank when she finds he wasn't paying attention, making him wonder \"...what makes that gal so <i>excitable</i>?\"\n\nIt's only another \"tale to astonish\" dressed up to shove the Wasp to the forefront."
  ],
